#: ../../../strings.rst:6
msgid "Subprocess Strings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:7
msgid "Strings in xonsh follow two simple rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:9
msgid "Strings in xonsh are always parsed in the same way, and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:10
msgid "Python always wins!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:12
msgid ""
"Together these rules mean that **even strings in subprocess mode are treated "
"like Python strings!** This will (help) preserve your sanity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:16
msgid "No Escape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:17
msgid ""
"Xonsh strings are exactly like Python strings everywhere. Xonsh uses exactly "
"the same escape characters that Python does; no more and no less."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:20
msgid "**bash**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:22
msgid ""
"$ echo A\\ Single\\ Argument\n"
"A Single Argument"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:27
msgid ""
"In the above example, since the spaces are escaped, the ``echo`` command "
"only receves a single argument. Xonsh does not allow this. If you were to "
"try this in xonsh, you'd see:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:31 ../../../strings.rst:70 ../../../strings.rst:96
#: ../../../strings.rst:109
msgid "**xonsh**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:33
msgid ""
"@ echo Actually\\ Three\\ Arguments\n"
"Actually\\ Three\\ Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:38
msgid ""
"In this example, echo recives three arguments:: ``\"Actually\\\\\"``, "
"``\"Three\\\\\"``, and ``\"Arguments\"``. Instead, xonsh requires you to use "
"quotes in order to pass in a single argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:42
msgid "**xonsh** or **bash**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:44
msgid ""
"$ echo \"A Single Argument\"\n"
"A Single Argument"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:49
msgid ""
"Using quotes is arguably what should have been done in sh-lang in the first "
"place."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:54
msgid "When in doubt in subprocess mode, use quotes!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:58
msgid "Justification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:59
msgid ""
"The reasons for not having additional escape sequences, as in sh-langs, are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:61
msgid "Escape charaters can get overwhemlingly ugly, fast."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:62
msgid "We have escape characters, they are called quotes :)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:63
msgid "We have literal input in subprocess mode via macros."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:65
msgid ""
"On this last point, if you don't already know about `Subprocess Macros "
"<macros.html#subprocess-macros>`_, these allow all input following an ``!`` "
"to be treated as a single argument. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:72
msgid ""
"@ echo! A  Single     Argument\n"
"A  Single     Argument"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:77
msgid "Subprocess macros are the ultimate escape mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:80
msgid "The Quotes Stay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:81
msgid "In sh-langs, internal quote characters are removed. For instance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:83
msgid ""
"$ echo foo\"bar\"baz\n"
"foobarbaz\n"
"\n"
"$ echo --key=\"value\"\n"
"--key=value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:91
msgid ""
"Xonsh considers this behavior suboptimal. Instead, xonsh treats these "
"arguments as if they were surrounded in another, outer level of quotation "
"(``'foo\"bar\"baz'``). Xonsh will keep the quotation marks when leading and "
"trailing quotes are not matched."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:98
msgid ""
"@ echo foo\"bar\"baz\n"
"foo\"bar\"baz\n"
"\n"
"@ echo --key=\"value\"\n"
"--key=\"value\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:106
msgid "You can think of these being equivalent to,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:111
msgid ""
"@ echo 'foo\"bar\"baz'\n"
"foo\"bar\"baz\n"
"\n"
"@ echo '--key=\"value\"'\n"
"--key=\"value\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:119
msgid ""
"This is yet another major point of departure for xonsh from traditional "
"shells. However, the xonsh subprocess string handling is consistent and "
"predictable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:124
msgid "Environment Variable Substitution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:126
msgid ""
"In subprocess mode, ``$NAME`` inside strings is replaced with the value of "
"the environment variable. This happens automatically for regular and f-"
"strings, but **not** for raw strings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:130
msgid ""
"echo $HOME\n"
"# /home/snail\n"
"\n"
"echo \"$HOME\"\n"
"# /home/snail\n"
"\n"
"echo r\"$HOME\"\n"
"# $HOME           -- raw strings suppress substitution\n"
"\n"
"echo $HOME/docs    # subprocess mode -- substituted inline\n"
"# /home/snail/docs\n"
"\n"
"del $MY_VAR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:146
msgid ""
"See `String Literal Prefixes`_ below for a full comparison of which string "
"types perform substitution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:151
msgid "String Literal Prefixes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:153
msgid ""
"For fine control of environment variable substitutions, brace substitutions, "
"and backslash escapes, xonsh supports an extended set of string literal "
"prefixes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:157
msgid ""
"``\"\"`` — regular string: backslash escapes. Envvar substitutions in "
"subprocess mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:158
msgid "``r\"\"`` — raw string: unmodified."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:159
msgid ""
"``f\"\"`` — formatted string: brace substitutions, backslash escapes. Envvar "
"substitutions in subprocess mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:160
msgid "``fr\"\"`` — raw formatted string: brace substitutions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:161
msgid ""
"``p\"\"`` — path string: backslash escapes, envvar substitutions, returns "
"Path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:162
msgid "``pr\"\"`` — raw path string: envvar substitutions, returns Path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:163
msgid ""
"``pf\"\"`` — formatted path string: backslash escapes, brace and envvar "
"substitutions, returns Path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:165
msgid ""
"To understand the differences, set ``$EVAR`` to ``1`` and ``var`` to ``2``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:170
msgid "String literal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:170
msgid "As python object"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:170
msgid "print(<String literal>)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:170
msgid "echo <String literal>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:172
msgid "``\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:172
msgid "``\"/$EVAR/'{var}'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:172
msgid "``/$EVAR/'{var}'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:172 ../../../strings.rst:176
msgid "``/1/'{var}'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:173
msgid "``r\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:173
msgid "``\"/$EVAR/\\\\'{var}\\\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:173
msgid "``/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:174
msgid "``f\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:174
msgid "``\"/$EVAR/'2'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:174
msgid "``/$EVAR/'2'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:174 ../../../strings.rst:178
msgid "``/1/'2'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:175
msgid "``fr\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:175
msgid "``\"/$EVAR/\\\\'2\\\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:175
msgid "``/$EVAR/\\'2\\'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:176
msgid "``p\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:176
msgid "``Path(\"/1/'{var}'\")``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:177
msgid "``pr\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:177
msgid "``Path(\"/1/\\\\'{var}\\\\'\")``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:177
msgid "``/1/\\'{var}\\'``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:178
msgid "``pf\"/$EVAR/\\'{var}\\'\"``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:178
msgid "``Path(\"/1/'2'\")``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:183
msgid "String Tricks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:186
msgid "Triple Quotes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:188
msgid ""
"To avoid escape characters (e.g. ``echo \"\\\"hello\\\"\"``), use triple "
"quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:191
msgid ""
"echo \"\"\"{\"hello\":'world'}\"\"\"\n"
"# {\"hello\":'world'}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:197
msgid "Creating Files with Multiline Strings"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:199
msgid ""
"Combine triple quotes with ``@()`` and a redirect to create files without "
"heredoc syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:202
msgid ""
"echo @(\"\"\"\n"
"line 1\n"
"line 2\n"
"line 3\n"
"\"\"\".strip()) > file.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:211
msgid "Multiline Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:213
msgid ""
"Use ``@()`` with triple quotes to pass a multiline string as a single "
"argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:216
msgid ""
"python -c @(\"\"\"\n"
"import sys\n"
"for i in range(3):\n"
"    print(i, sys.platform)\n"
"\"\"\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:225
msgid "f-strings in Commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:227
msgid ""
"You can use ``@()`` to inject an f-string, but f-strings also work directly "
"in subprocess mode:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:230
msgid ""
"echo @(f'Hello {$HOME}')\n"
"# Hello /home/snail\n"
"\n"
"# f-strings work by themselves too:\n"
"echo f'Hello {$HOME}'\n"
"# Hello /home/snail"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:241
msgid "Splitting Strings Like the Shell"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:243
msgid ""
"Xonsh's :meth:`Lexer.split() <xonsh.parsers.lexer.Lexer.split>` can split a "
"string into tokens the same way the shell does -- respecting quoting, "
"escapes, and operators.  This is the function used internally by the "
"``@$()`` operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../strings.rst:248
msgid ""
"from xonsh.parsers.lexer import Lexer\n"
"Lexer().split('echo \"hello world\" file.txt')\n"
"# ['echo', '\"hello world\"', 'file.txt']"
msgstr ""

